    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Philosophically, opponents frequently invoke the concept of legal liability, arguing that a claim requires a direct cause-and-effect link between the original harm and the present-day suffering, which they believe is too attenuated in time. A major legal counterargument revolves around statutes of limitations and the impossibility of assigning current individuals criminal or civil culpability for historical actions. Some also raise issues of practical justice, questioning how a one-time payment can fairly compensate for generations of systemic disenfranchisement and ongoing structural inequality.",
      "source": "target",
